{"kind":"assert","text":"AS-1 report (request de57d72b, promise e39df770). Source only, at main d3896041. I ran nothing and read only the NF-8 attempt 1 log, plus the log of the run of the case alone.\n\n1. The path. The driver prints archive-pass-diagnostic-v1 for every pass. For seq 9780 it says: step send, rowPhaseAtLoad confirmed, confirmed 9779, attemptsAtLoad 0, state retry, reason unavailable, caught unavailable, capacityRefusal null. The pass had written its Mark and its attempt (archive-driver.ts:492-499) and minted a credential (503-507). It then stopped inside budget.wait() around provider.sendArchive (archive-driver.ts:510). The catch at archive-driver.ts:539-540 returned {retry, next: scheduled, unavailable}; next was clock + 60 s, which is #later(0) at archive-driver.ts:163-168. Within wait(), three things can turn the send into ArchiveStopped(\"unavailable\"):\n (a) check() saw the clock go backwards: archive-budget.ts:73, called before and after the work at :245. The case builds its budget with the default clock, performance.now (archive-budget.ts:58).\n (b) The send threw something other than ArchiveStopped, which wait() turns into unavailable at archive-budget.ts:246-249. Sources include GitRefusal from bad() (archive-send.ts:10), SmartHttpGit, the fixture's own expect() calls inside sendArchive (test/support/archive-provider.ts:49-50 and 64-68), and runtime errors.\n (c) verifyJournalAppend answered unavailable (archive-send.ts:34-35).\nThe log does not say which, because wait() keeps no record of the cause it converts. The run of the case alone and gate attempt 2 confirmed seq 9780 with the same inputs, so a deterministic data check is unlikely. That leaves (a) or a transient throw under (b). In attempt 1 the case shared the Native scale pool with fixed-slot-membership; their output interleaves in the log. This is an inference, not a finding.\n\n2. A deployed room. Yes, it can take this path. The production driver and wait() are the same code, and a failed send leaves its Mark in place. The wake under AR-1: run() calls #retryWake (archive-driver.ts:142, :152-160). This keeps the stored wake that attempt() wrote, now + 60 s × 2^attempts, which is 60 s for a first attempt. The next pass resolves the Mark through pending-readback (archive-driver.ts:304-326). If the remote ref already holds the commit, that pass confirms with no new send. Otherwise it sends again at attempts 1 (120 s), then 240 s and so on, capped at 3600 s (archive-driver.ts:94, :167). So one transient costs one wake after 60 s. A persistent cause settles at one wake per hour per room, the AR-1 bound.\n\n3. Which is wrong. The driver does what it is designed to do. A send whose outcome is unknown must retry and let read-back decide. The case expects confirmed from every one of 10,001 passes, so any transient fails it. The real defect is diagnosability: the pass names the step but not which check stopped it. Proposal, as separate requests: (i) record the stop cause in the diagnostic (clock regression, the error name of a thrown error, or the verifier's reason) at archive-budget.ts:246-249 and :73; (ii) then decide, with that evidence, whether the case should allow one retry that the next pass confirms by read-back, or whether the cause is a fault to fix. Your call. No code change in this lane.","body":{"no_git_artifact":"true","to":"20db43a245e9780b92a587425d387d393b17ffc90b89e036ff752399f88fc517"}}